{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-137182","model_call":7,"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"social engineering\" (+ \"psychological manipulation\" manipulate) (+ victim victims) (+ emotions trust fear urgency curiosity))"},"record":{"query":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"social engineering\" (+ \"psychological manipulation\" manipulate) (+ victim victims) (+ emotions trust fear urgency curiosity))"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_44_1480493115#5_3062054717","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q1991","rank":1,"snippet":"19 Smishing Financial Services Smishing Gift Smishing Invoice or Order Confirmation Smishing Customer Support Smishing Smishing examples Early Access Apple iPhone 12 Scam — Order Confirm & Gift Smishing USPS and FedEx Scams — Order Confirmation & Gift Smishing Mandatory Online COVID-19 Test Scam — COVID-19 Smishing How to prevent Smishing What to do if you become a victim of Smishing \" , \"segment\": \"How does Smishing work? Deception and fraud are the core components of any SMS phishing attack. As the attacker assumes an identity that you might <cover>trust, you are more likely to succumb to their requests. Social engineering principles allow smishing attackers to manipulate a victim’</cover>s decision-making. The driving factors of this deception are three-fold: Trust: By posing as legitimate individuals and organizations, cybercriminals lower their target’s skepticism. SMS texts, as a more personal communication channel, also naturally lower a person’s defenses against threats. Context: Using a situation that could be relevant to targets allows an attacker to build an effective disguise.\" , \"start_char\": 2640.000000 , \"title\": \"What is Smishing & How to Defend Against it | Kaspersky\" , \""},"time":411},"model_calls":7,"document_judgments":4,"relevance_3":2,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
