{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-137182","model_call":9,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1991"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1991"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_40_1107020185#1_2331556541","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1991","rank":3,"snippet":"global leader in electronic fraud protection. Share to Facebook Share to Twitter Share to Linkedin Shutterstock It’s 6 p.m. on a Friday, and one of your clients gets an email from your company stating there has been unusual activity on their account and that a password reset is required. Employing security best practices, the user verifies that the email address has the name of your company (which he or she does business with all the time), the email format looks familiar and the website is the same as always. Your client proceeds to enter their old password to confirm their identity and then enters a new one. Fast-forward to Monday morning’s news headlines: Your company and your client have fallen victim to a massive phishing scheme. How Did This Happen? Phishing is all about the art of deception. Through <cover>social engineering, attackers take advantage human curiosity, fear and gullibility to manipulate their victims.</cover>\" , \"start_char\": 906.000000 , \"title\": \"The Dangers Of Phishing\" , \"url\": \"https://www.forbes.com/sites/forbestechcouncil/2017/09/14/the-dangers-of-phishing/\" }"},"time":15},"model_calls":9,"document_judgments":6,"relevance_3":4,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
