{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-137182","model_call":29,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1991"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1991"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_40_1246643094#6_2667012999","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1991","rank":23,"snippet":"Safe Device Use Habits Use Comprehensive Internet Security Software Do Not Ever Leave Your Devices Unsecured in Public Keep All Software Updated Check for Known Data Breaches of Your Online Accounts How Fortinet Can Help FAQs What is social engineering? How does social engineering work? What are the well-known examples of <cover>social engineering attacks? Learn more about Fortinet today \" , \"segment\": \"They then either get them to click on a malicious link or provide private information via a form. Spear Phishing With a spear-phishing attack, the victim is specifically targeted, and the attacker often performs extensive research ahead of time. Once the attacker knows how to manipulate the victim, they launch the attack, phishing for information, credentials, or sensitive data. Water Holing With water holing, the attacker tries to compromise a targeted group of individuals by infecting sites they trust. </cover>The attacker may focus on sites that the people visit frequently, knowing they are likely to feel safe on those pages. Quid Pro Quo In a quid pro quo attack, the attacker pretends to provide something to the victim in exchange for information or a specific action. For example, the attacker may pretend "},"time":16},"model_calls":29,"document_judgments":26,"relevance_3":23,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
