{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-137182","model_call":35,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1992"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1992"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_40_1246643094#4_2667007090","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1992","rank":2,"snippet":"A baiting attack attempts to draw in a victim by promising something that appeals to their sense of curiosity or greed. This lures the target into installing or clicking on something that ends up putting malware, such as that used for pharming or spyware, onto their system. Scareware Scareware bombards a target with fake threats or false alarms in the hopes that their natural inclination to protect themselves or something they value drives them to taking the desired action. One of the more common types is using realistic-looking banners warning that their computer may be infected with a virus or some other kind of malware. Pretexting In an attack that uses pretexting, the attacker lies to the victim regarding their identity. After they have gained the target’s trust, they trick them into handing over sensitive information. Phishing In a <cover>phishing attack, the attacker creates a sense of urgency or appeals to the victim’</cover>s curiosity.\" , \"start_char\": 2259.000000 , \"title\": \"What Is Social Engineering? Preventing Social Engineering Attacks | Fortinet\" , \"url\": \"https://www.fortinet.com/resources/cyberglossary/social-engineering\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_40_1246643094#5_2667010111","rank":1,"relevance":3}]},"time":8},"model_calls":35,"document_judgments":32,"relevance_3":28,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
