{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-137182","model_call":41,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1993"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1993"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_37_409947886#0_858246067","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1993","rank":5,"snippet":"sensitive personal information over the phone. By Josh Fruhlinger CSO | May 18, 2020 7:40 am PDT Tero Vesalainen / WhataWin / Getty Images What is vishing? Vishing is a form of attack that attempts to trick victims into giving up sensitive personal information over the phone. While that makes it sound like an old-fashioned scam, vishing attacks have high-tech elements: they involve automated voice simulation technology, for instance, or the scammer may use personal information about the victim harvested from earlier cyberattacks to put them at ease. No matter what technology is used, the setup for the attack follows a familiar social engineering script: An attacker creates a scenario to prey on human <cover>emotions, commonly greed or fear, and convinces the victim to disclose sensitive information, like credit card numbers or passwords. In that sense, vishing techniques mirror the phishing </cover>scams that have been around since the 1990s.\" , \"start_char\": 0.000000 , \"title\": \"What is vishing? How voice phishing scams victims | CSO Online\" , \"url\": \"https://www.csoonline.com/article/3543771/vishing-explained-how-voice-phishing-attacks-scam-victims.html\" }"},"time":9},"model_calls":41,"document_judgments":37,"relevance_3":33,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
